question:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R}^{+} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{+}$ such that

$$
f(x+f(y))=f(x+y)+f(y)\tag{1}
$$

for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$. (Symbol $\mathbb{R}^{+}$denotes the set of all positive real numbers.)

response:
First, we see that $f(1)$ must be $1$, since

\begin{align*}
f\left(f(x)^{2}\right) &= f\left(f(x)^{2} \cdot 1\right) = x^{3} f(x) \\
f\left(f(1)^{2} x\right) &= f\left(f(1) \cdot f(1) x\right) = 1^{3} f(x) = f(x)
\end{align*}

Thus, $f(x) = f\left(f(1)^{2} x\right)$ for all $x$, so $f(1)^{2} = 1$. Since $f(1) \in \mathbb{Q}^{+}$, we have $f(1) = 1$.

Now, let $x = 1$ in (1) to get

\begin{align*}
f\left(f(y)\right) &= f\left(f(1)^{2} y\right) = 1^{3} f(y) = f(y)
\end{align*}

So, $f$ is an injective function.

Since $f$ is injective, we can apply $f$ to both sides of (1) to get

\begin{align*}
f\left(f\left(f(x)^{2} y\right)\right) &= f\left(x^{3} f(x y)\right) \\
f\left(x^{3} f(x y)\right) &= f\left(f(x)^{2} y\right)
\end{align*}

Now, let $y = 1$ in (1) to get

\begin{align*}
f\left(f(x)^{2}\right) &= x^{3} f(x)
\end{align*}

Substituting $f(x) = x^{3} f(x)$ into the equation above, we get

\begin{align*}
f\left(x^{3} f(x)\right) &= f\left(f(x)^{2}\right) = x^{3} f(x)
\end{align*}

Since $f$ is injective, we can conclude that $f(x) = x^{3} f(x)$, so $f(x) = x$.
</think>

<answer>\boxed{f(x)=x}</answer>

