\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{B(x)}&=\frac{1}{x}-\frac{1}{A(x)}-\frac{1}{x+10}\\
&=\frac{A(x)(x+10)-x(x+10)-A(x)}{x(x+10)A(x)}\\
&=\frac{A(x)(x+9)-10x}{x(x+10)A(x)}
\end{align*}
Thus,
\begin{align*}
B(x)&=\frac{x(x+10)A(x)}{A(x)(x+9)-10x}\\
&=\frac{x(x+10)(x^n+c_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\dots+c_1x+c_0)}{x^n+(c_{n-1}+9)x^{n-1}+\dots+(c_1-10)x+c_0}
\end{align*}
where $n$ is the degree of $A(x)$ and $c_i$ are its coefficients. Note that $B(x)$ is a polynomial if and only if the denominator divides the numerator. Since the numerator and denominator have the same degree, it must be that the denominator is a constant multiple of the numerator, which means that $c_{n-1}+9=0$ and $c_i-10=0$ for $i=1,2,\dots,n-1$. Therefore, $c_i=10$ for $i=1,2,\dots,n-1$ and $c_{n-1}=-9$, so that $A(x)$ must be of the form $x^n+10x^{n-1}+\dots+10x-9$.

